      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. I understand you've confirmed it, but 5,100 mg/day is twice the maximum daily dose. The reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visit and evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for exceeding that limit. The risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI effects is real, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** For example, 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day), or a lower starting dose with titration. I can enter either one right away.\n- **Talk to pharmacy or the attending.** If there's a clinical reason for going above the maximum, they can review it. The order can then be entered with their documented approval.\n- **Change the timeline.** Metformin is usually titrated over days to weeks, so the order doesn't need to reach a final dose before this afternoon. I can write a note on the order about planned titration for the family and rounds.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
